WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.422 --> 00:00:04.922
- Ultimately, all music is
or can be inspirational.

00:00:04.922 --> 00:00:07.268
Fanbase I have now,
they're so sophisticated,

00:00:07.268 --> 00:00:09.710
they almost expect me
to do the unexpected.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.333
I don't look at myself
through other people's eyes.

00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:14.813
One of my favorite bandleaders
is Bruce Springsteen.

00:00:14.813 --> 00:00:16.662
I've watched him many years.

00:00:16.662 --> 00:00:19.588
I don't wallow in it, I
don't spend time in a place,

00:00:19.588 --> 00:00:21.338
I let myself move on.

00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:24.446
- Yeah.

00:00:24.446 --> 00:00:25.595
- There's enough things
that bring you down,

00:00:25.595 --> 00:00:29.198
we don't need to jack our
music up that way, too.

00:00:29.198 --> 00:00:32.278
I don't really care so much
what people say about me,

00:00:32.278 --> 00:00:36.187
because it usually is a
reflection of who they are.

00:00:36.187 --> 00:00:38.475
I used him as a role model in trying

00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:40.660
to play all the instruments.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.015
I kind of wish we got back
to the days where the music

00:00:44.015 --> 00:00:47.518
was more like the soul
music of the '60s and '70s.

00:00:47.518 --> 00:00:48.533
- Yeah, when was--

00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:49.451
- Born--
- Yeah.

00:00:49.451 --> 00:00:50.508
- On a certain day,

00:00:50.508 --> 00:00:52.416
you had no more birthdays after that.

00:00:52.416 --> 00:00:54.788
So I don't celebrate birthdays.

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:56.776
- He was an American singer, songwriter,

00:00:56.776 --> 00:00:59.308
record producer, actor, and artist.

00:00:59.308 --> 00:01:01.404
He was an innovator in the music industry

00:01:01.404 --> 00:01:03.735
and was known for his flamboyant style

00:01:03.735 --> 00:01:05.581
and wide musical range.

00:01:05.581 --> 00:01:08.067
He sold over 100 million
records worldwide,

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.632
making him one of the most
popular recording artists

00:01:10.632 --> 00:01:12.129
of all time.

00:01:12.129 --> 00:01:15.343
He's Prince, and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:15.343 --> 00:01:16.428
(swoosh)

00:01:16.428 --> 00:01:18.249
(thunk)

00:01:18.249 --> 00:01:21.249
- How would you describe your music?

00:01:22.977 --> 00:01:24.434
- Hmm...

00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:27.188
- [Larry] What idiom would you put it in?

00:01:27.188 --> 00:01:28.563
- The only thing I could think of,

00:01:28.563 --> 00:01:30.087
'cause I really don't like categories,

00:01:30.087 --> 00:01:31.073
but the only thing I could think

00:01:31.073 --> 00:01:33.006
of is inspirational and I think music

00:01:33.006 --> 00:01:35.173
that is from the heart falls right

00:01:35.173 --> 00:01:36.621
into that category.

00:01:36.621 --> 00:01:38.512
People really feel what it is

00:01:38.512 --> 00:01:40.595
that they're doing and...

00:01:43.816 --> 00:01:47.899
Ultimately, all music is
or can be inspirational.

00:01:49.517 --> 00:01:53.139
That's why it's so important
to let your gift be guided

00:01:53.139 --> 00:01:55.413
by something more clear.

00:01:55.413 --> 00:01:58.015
Well, first of all, I
don't know who was the one

00:01:58.015 --> 00:01:59.833
that came up with the notion that you have

00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:01.989
to play the same songs every concert.

00:02:01.989 --> 00:02:03.242
- Probably fans.

00:02:03.242 --> 00:02:07.170
- Yeah, well.
- (laughs)

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.611
- Most of the people that come see us now,

00:02:08.611 --> 00:02:12.172
they've been to see me umpteen times.

00:02:12.172 --> 00:02:16.720
And the only pity is when
they bring their kids

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.321
and they're trying to show their kids

00:02:18.321 --> 00:02:20.321
the experience they had.

00:02:21.208 --> 00:02:22.898
But I don't know how any of us grow

00:02:22.898 --> 00:02:24.815
if we just tread water.

00:02:25.652 --> 00:02:28.402
The idea is that we keep growing,

00:02:29.794 --> 00:02:32.787
like we were saying before is the fanbase

00:02:32.787 --> 00:02:34.859
I have now is so sophisticated,

00:02:34.859 --> 00:02:37.711
they almost expect me
to do the unexpected.

00:02:37.711 --> 00:02:41.021
And that gives me a lot of
room to challenge myself

00:02:41.021 --> 00:02:42.806
as well as them.

00:02:42.806 --> 00:02:46.091
I don't look at myself
through other people's eyes.

00:02:46.091 --> 00:02:48.895
I had an interesting
discussion with Larry Graham,

00:02:48.895 --> 00:02:52.895
a journalist upstairs,
about criticism of music.

00:02:54.011 --> 00:02:56.513
If you're a true artist,
you're using the gift

00:02:56.513 --> 00:02:58.284
you've been given from God.

00:02:58.284 --> 00:02:59.117
- Yeah.

00:03:00.359 --> 00:03:04.526
- To criticize a gift from God
is sort of to criticize God.

00:03:05.802 --> 00:03:06.635
- Yeah.

00:03:06.635 --> 00:03:08.619
- Now you can cut that any way you want,

00:03:08.619 --> 00:03:11.788
but (laughs) it's the truth, so...

00:03:11.788 --> 00:03:12.843
- Yeah.

00:03:12.843 --> 00:03:14.118
- I looked at the writer,

00:03:14.118 --> 00:03:16.681
I said I wouldn't criticize
your writing here,

00:03:16.681 --> 00:03:20.177
I'm not a writer, I don't
write books, I wouldn't...

00:03:20.177 --> 00:03:22.196
- I was reading that you'll go out there

00:03:22.196 --> 00:03:24.265
with 300 songs that you might decide

00:03:24.265 --> 00:03:26.704
at the last minute to perform.

00:03:26.704 --> 00:03:29.019
That's very risky, is it not?

00:03:29.019 --> 00:03:31.537
The whole band has to be
in sync with you, right?

00:03:31.537 --> 00:03:35.704
- Well, they rehearse a lot,
it's a well-oiled machine.

00:03:36.922 --> 00:03:39.839
One of my favorite band
leaders is Bruce Springsteen,

00:03:39.839 --> 00:03:41.581
I've watched him many years

00:03:41.581 --> 00:03:43.998
and I was backstage one night

00:03:45.363 --> 00:03:48.605
and I saw him turn around and give a cue

00:03:48.605 --> 00:03:50.596
and the band switched on a dime.

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:53.961
I used to see James Brown do that a lot.

00:03:53.961 --> 00:03:55.138
You learn from the best.

00:03:55.138 --> 00:03:59.092
I've always known that God was my creator

00:03:59.092 --> 00:04:02.092
and that without Him, nothing works.

00:04:07.448 --> 00:04:10.779
It works to a point and then
it just kind of deteriorates,

00:04:10.779 --> 00:04:12.509
entropy takes place.

00:04:12.509 --> 00:04:14.259
- [Larry] When bad things
have happened to you,

00:04:14.259 --> 00:04:15.644
do you blame Him?

00:04:15.644 --> 00:04:17.632
- No, absolutely not, no.

00:04:17.632 --> 00:04:20.407
- How do you explain, how do
you resolve that in yourself?

00:04:20.407 --> 00:04:24.521
- I learn from it and
I don't wallow in it,

00:04:24.521 --> 00:04:27.027
I don't spend time in a place.

00:04:27.027 --> 00:04:28.777
I let myself move on.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:34.705
Right today, I could sit and say

00:04:34.705 --> 00:04:37.146
I have animosity towards--

00:04:37.146 --> 00:04:37.979
- [Larry] A record company.

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:41.535
- Yeah, a company that
owns the rights to my work.

00:04:41.535 --> 00:04:44.286
They're businessmen,
they're doing what it is

00:04:44.286 --> 00:04:46.562
that makes their business successful.

00:04:46.562 --> 00:04:48.428
And I'm also allowed to do things

00:04:48.428 --> 00:04:51.148
that make my business successful.

00:04:51.148 --> 00:04:53.318
And for me, that would be to own my work.

00:04:53.318 --> 00:04:57.226
So I just chose to step away
from that and knowing that,

00:04:57.226 --> 00:05:00.141
I sent a nice letter
to the then-president,

00:05:00.141 --> 00:05:02.761
cause they changed a lot,
weekly, during that time--

00:05:02.761 --> 00:05:03.671
- (chuckles)

00:05:03.671 --> 00:05:05.645
- I told him that I loved him,

00:05:05.645 --> 00:05:07.233
and that I was glad

00:05:07.233 --> 00:05:09.077
that I had this experience.

00:05:09.077 --> 00:05:12.191
- Cause your music, it
gets ya, but in a good way

00:05:12.191 --> 00:05:14.209
and I think that's what
music should always do.

00:05:14.209 --> 00:05:15.850
- I think it should uplift--

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:16.683
- Yeah.

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:18.396
- There's enough things
that bring you down,

00:05:18.396 --> 00:05:22.212
we don't need to jack our
music up that way, too.

00:05:22.212 --> 00:05:25.545
I mean, there's still a way to get anger

00:05:27.369 --> 00:05:31.009
and even hate across sometimes, in music.

00:05:31.009 --> 00:05:33.687
But you have to resolve
it, you have to show

00:05:33.687 --> 00:05:36.854
that it's useless to hold these ideas.

00:05:38.275 --> 00:05:41.393
I don't really care so much
what people say about me,

00:05:41.393 --> 00:05:45.366
because it usually is a
reflection of who they are.

00:05:45.366 --> 00:05:47.727
For example, if people wish I would sound

00:05:47.727 --> 00:05:49.737
like I used to sound,

00:05:49.737 --> 00:05:52.787
then it says more about
them than it does me.

00:05:52.787 --> 00:05:55.454
If I change the lyrics to a song

00:05:57.022 --> 00:05:58.558
then that begs the question

00:05:58.558 --> 00:06:00.496
why do you want me to curse?

00:06:00.496 --> 00:06:02.749
Why do you want me to
talk like other people?

00:06:02.749 --> 00:06:06.670
See, cursing was cool
when nobody was doing it.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:07.503
- (laughs)

00:06:07.503 --> 00:06:08.766
- Or just a couple people.

00:06:08.766 --> 00:06:09.599
- Yeah.

00:06:09.599 --> 00:06:12.259
- Yeah, like if everybody
wears the same clothes,

00:06:12.259 --> 00:06:14.092
it ain't cool no more.

00:06:14.966 --> 00:06:16.916
You're trying to be different.

00:06:16.916 --> 00:06:19.966
One can't be different
by being racy today.

00:06:19.966 --> 00:06:21.942
It's not interesting anymore.

00:06:21.942 --> 00:06:26.001
Sexiness was in the mind,
it was in your imagination.

00:06:26.001 --> 00:06:29.200
When you lose that, then, like you said,

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.378
it's just old skin.

00:06:30.378 --> 00:06:32.868
I use Stevie Wonder as an inspiration,

00:06:32.868 --> 00:06:35.476
whom I look up to a great deal

00:06:35.476 --> 00:06:38.643
just for the way that he crafted music

00:06:40.079 --> 00:06:43.495
and his connection to the spirit.

00:06:43.495 --> 00:06:45.912
And boy, back then I used him

00:06:48.742 --> 00:06:49.852
as a role model

00:06:49.852 --> 00:06:52.912
in trying to play all the instruments

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:56.491
and be very self-contained
and keep my vision clear.

00:06:56.491 --> 00:06:59.694
So word spread very quickly
about what I could do.

00:06:59.694 --> 00:07:01.381
A lot of people knew about it.

00:07:01.381 --> 00:07:05.351
I meet a lot of artists
but the record industry,

00:07:05.351 --> 00:07:06.184
they tend to promote things

00:07:06.184 --> 00:07:08.684
that are more salacious and...

00:07:12.414 --> 00:07:14.775
hit-driven, I guess,

00:07:14.775 --> 00:07:15.942
to use a term.

00:07:19.854 --> 00:07:21.657
I kind of wish we got back to the days

00:07:21.657 --> 00:07:25.342
where the music was
more like the soul music

00:07:25.342 --> 00:07:27.092
of the '60s and '70s.

00:07:30.744 --> 00:07:32.446
Alicia Keyes, I dig.

00:07:32.446 --> 00:07:34.681
I think she's kind of
puttin' it out there,

00:07:34.681 --> 00:07:35.597
there's a few out there.

00:07:35.597 --> 00:07:38.272
- In other words, the lyrics
were what made you feel sexy,

00:07:38.272 --> 00:07:40.693
not the fact that you
had barely anything on.

00:07:40.693 --> 00:07:44.860
- Yeah, yeah, it's a reality
show, nowadays, so...

00:07:46.204 --> 00:07:49.907
Once a person gets a firm
grip of life and death,

00:07:49.907 --> 00:07:51.746
then they fully understand both.

00:07:51.746 --> 00:07:55.430
For example, you had how many birthdays?

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:56.989
- Me, what do you think?

00:07:56.989 --> 00:08:00.116
- Well, I know that you had one birthday.

00:08:00.116 --> 00:08:00.949
- Yeah, when was--

00:08:00.949 --> 00:08:01.782
- Born--

00:08:01.782 --> 00:08:02.615
- Yeah.

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:03.448
- On a certain day,

00:08:03.448 --> 00:08:04.866
you had no more birthdays after that.

00:08:04.866 --> 00:08:07.017
So I don't celebrate birthdays.

00:08:07.017 --> 00:08:09.376
So that stops me from counting days,

00:08:09.376 --> 00:08:11.267
which stops me from counting time,

00:08:11.267 --> 00:08:12.862
which allows me to still look the same

00:08:12.862 --> 00:08:16.261
as I did 10 years ago,
just like that man said.

00:08:16.261 --> 00:08:18.511
(cheering)

00:08:20.846 --> 00:08:22.110
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:25.916
I made this video because
D.J. El Magnifico asked me to,

00:08:25.916 --> 00:08:27.328
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:08:27.328 --> 00:08:28.765
that you want me to profile next,

00:08:28.765 --> 00:08:30.024
leave it in the comments below

00:08:30.024 --> 00:08:31.111
and I'll see what I can do.

00:08:31.111 --> 00:08:32.290
I'd also love to know which of

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:34.682
Prince's top 10 rules
meant the most to you,

00:08:34.682 --> 00:08:35.897
leave it in the comments

00:08:35.897 --> 00:08:37.076
and I'll join the discussion.

00:08:37.076 --> 00:08:38.872
Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:08:38.872 --> 00:08:41.607
continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

00:08:41.607 --> 00:08:42.701
(swoosh)

00:08:42.701 --> 00:08:44.093
(thunk)

